<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyushu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kyūshū</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyushu is the third-largest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s main islands, lying south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> and closest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It was an early gateway for continental culture and, in the 16th century, for Portuguese traders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Its great warrior houses, such as the Shimazu, Ōtomo and Ryūzōji, fought for control until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> conquered the island in 1587.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyushu.jpg" alt="Kyushu" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terra_Kyushu_20091028.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kyushu is the most southerly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s four main islands, with a land area of 36,782 km2. It is mountainous, and its highest point is Mount Kujū at 1,791 m. Mount Aso, at 1,591 m, is the largest active volcano in Japan, and tectonic activity also shows in many hot springs, the best known of them at Beppu on the east shore and around Aso in the centre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanmon-straits">Kanmon Straits</a> separate the island from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>. Because it is the island nearest the Asian continent, it has historically been the gateway to Japan. Parts of Kyushu, particularly Miyazaki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a> prefectures, have a subtropical climate, and the main crops are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a> and soy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kyushu has been called Kyūkoku, the Nine Countries, Chinzei, meaning West of the Pacified Area, and Tsukushi-no-shima. The historical region of Saikaidō, the West Sea Circuit, covered the island and its surroundings, and the present name Kyūshū, nine provinces, comes from the nine ancient provinces on it: Chikuzen, Chikugo, Hizen, Higo, Buzen, Bungo, Hyūga, Ōsumi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiho-code">Taihō Code</a> reforms of the 8th century, Dazaifu was established as a special administrative term for the region. One theory holds that in ancient times Kyushu was home to its own independent dynasty, with a southern-influenced culture and tradition distinct from that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kyushu holds about 10 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s population, concentrated along the northwest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> and Kitakyushu, with corridors running to Sasebo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a>. It has maintained the strongest economic and cultural ties with Okinawa from ancient times, and traces of Okinawan scales, cuisine and language appear throughout. The gottan, a traditional stringed instrument, is regarded as a midpoint between the shamisen of mainland Japan and the Okinawan sanshin. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">Porcelain</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arita">Arita</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imari">Imari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and Karatsu is well known, and the Tanegashima Space Center is Japan's largest rocket-launch complex. The island has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> including Yakushima, the Sites of Japan's Meiji Industrial Revolution, and the Hidden Christian Sites in the Nagasaki Region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kyushu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1281 some 50,000 Mongol, Korean and Chinese troops and about 100,000 conscripts from the defeated Song fought for some seven weeks in <b>Kyushu</b> without strategic gain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kyushu</b> remained about 190 km from the Korean Peninsula, close enough for occasional continental influence but far enough for the islands to develop on their own.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the traditional Yayoi chronology, Mumun-like settlements appeared in northern <b>Kyūshū</b> during the Late Mumun.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mumun-culture">Mumun culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The golden seal that Emperor Guangwu of the Later Han dynasty gave to the Na state of Wo in 57 CE, recorded in the Book of the Later Han, was found in northern <b>Kyushu</b> in the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yayoi-culture">Yayoi culture · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Susanoo slays the eight-headed serpent Yamata-no-Orochi, Ōkuninushi rules the earthly land until the heavenly deities pacify it, and Ninigi, Amaterasu's grandson, descends to <b>Kyūshū</b> as the progenitor of the imperial line.</blockquote><a href="/work/kojiki">Kojiki · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Hideyoshi's invasion of Shikoku in 1585 reduced Motochika to Tosa, and his heir Nobuchika died in the <b>Kyushu</b> campaign in 1586.</blockquote><a href="/state/chosokabe-clan-domain">Chosokabe clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
